  "content": {
    "id": "p47-q22-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "The curve $y=ax^2+bx+4$ has a stationary point at $(3,-5)$. Find $a$ and $b$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=1$ and $b=-6$.",
      "worked": "At the stationary point the gradient is zero. Since $y'=2ax+b$, substituting $x=3$ gives $$\\begin{align*}6a+b&=0\\\\b&=-6a.\\end{align*}$$ Substituting $(3,-5)$ into the curve gives $$\\begin{align*}-5&=9a+3b+4\\\\-5&=9a+3(-6a)+4\\\\-5&=-9a+4\\\\9a&=9\\\\a&=1.\\end{align*}$$ Then $b=-6(1)=-6$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
